<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pierre Werner</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1913">1913</a>–<a href="/years/2002">2002</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pierre Werner was a Luxembourg Christian Social politician who served as prime minister from 1959 to 1974 and again from 1979 to 1984. In 1970 he chaired the group that produced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/werner-report">Werner Report</a>, the first plan for economic and monetary union in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-communities">European Communities</a>, which is why he is often called a father of the euro. His long tenure saw the diversification of Luxembourg's economy into banking and services.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pierre-werner.jpg" alt="Pierre Werner" width="320" height="444" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Marcel Tockert · CC BY-SA 3.0 lu · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pierre_Werner_204g.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Pierre Werner was born on 29 December 1913 at Saint-André-lez-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lille">Lille</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxembourgish">Luxembourgish</a> parents. He studied at the Cours Supérieurs de Luxembourg in 1933 and 1934, then at the Law Faculty of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-paris">University of Paris</a> and the École libre des sciences politiques until 1937, and completed a doctorate in law in Luxembourg in 1938.</p><p>He chaired the Association of Catholic Students from 1935 to 1937. After a short time as a lawyer in Luxembourg City he became a banker. During the Nazi occupation of 1940 to 1945 he gave clandestine support to the resistance. After the war he became Controller of the banking system and attended the Bretton Woods Conference, which set up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/international-monetary-fund">International Monetary Fund</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>Werner became Finance Minister in 1953 and was the 19th and 21st prime minister, titled President of the Government, from 1959 to 1974 and from 1979 to 1984. He governed in coalition with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/democratic-party-uganda">Democratic Party</a> from 1959 to 1964, with the Luxembourg Socialist Workers' Party from 1964 to 1969, and again with the Democratic Party from 1969 to 1974.</p><p>The Christian Social People's Party went into opposition from 1974 to 1979, during which time he led it in parliament. His last government was another coalition with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/democratic-party-uganda">Democratic Party</a>. In 1965 he helped end the empty chair crisis by persuading <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, whose president <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-de-gaulle">Charles de Gaulle</a> had suspended its participation in European meetings, to resume its seat.</p><p>In 1970 the heads of state or government gave Werner the mandate to draft, with a group of experts, a blueprint for an economic and monetary union within the EEC. His report advocated gradual reforms, the irreversible fixing of exchange rates and a single currency within a decade. It was not implemented at the time, owing to internal political disagreements and the oil crisis, but Jacques Delors later revived it, and its principles were enshrined in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maastricht-treaty">Treaty of Maastricht</a>, which paved the way for the euro.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>At home Werner worked to diversify an economy hit by a Europe-wide crisis in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a> industry, attracting new industrial investment and financial services. He is remembered for using tripartite mediation between industry, labour and government to overcome the steel crisis of 1979 to 1984.</p><p>He negotiated the relocation of several European institutions to Luxembourg and, after leaving politics in 1984, chaired the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ses-s-a">Société Européenne des Satellites</a> from 1989 to 1996, having laid the ground for the company. He sponsored culture, especially music, and promoted the restoration of Vianden Castle. He died in Luxembourg City on 24 June 2002. The Pierre Werner Institute was created in Luxembourg in 2003, and the country's main cricket ground at Walferdange is named after him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pierre Werner” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Long-serving prime ministers such as <b>Pierre Werner</b> and Jean-Claude Juncker have dominated politics.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-luxembourg">Grand Duchy of Luxembourg · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Saint-André-lez-Lille, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in Luxembourg. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.66,3.04&amp;year=1970" aria-label="Saint-André-lez-Lille on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4619 405 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Pierre Werner"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5069 675h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5069 675h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=50.66,3.04&amp;year=1970">What was here in 1970? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
